// The DECLARED ownership of a `let` decides what the binding holds. The
// initializer only says WHICH OBJECT.
//
// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS BESIDE write_through.cell AND write_through_named.cell.
// write_through.cell measures the five unique-borrow spellings in the ARGUMENT
// position, `grow(exclusive buf)`. write_through_named.cell measures three of
// them parked in a name first, and its header says plainly that it can carry
// only three, because the C backend broke the other two in a `let` initializer
// and pinning either answer would have blessed a wrong one. This file is the
// completion of that: all five unique spellings AND all three shared spellings
// in a `let`, plus the `copy`-of-a-borrow shape, now that the reference backend
// agrees with the other two about every one of them.
//
// WHAT WENT WRONG, measured at ea36e3d. `codegen.letType` derived an
// un-annotated binding's C type from `inferExpr(initializer)`, consulting the
// declared ownership for `arc` alone. So the SPELLING decided whether the
// binding was a reference, and the spellings disagreed with each other and with
// `borrowck.checkLetInit`, which creates one identical named loan for all of
// them:
//
//     let exclusive e = exclusive buf     cell_Buffer e = buf;          COPY
//     let exclusive e = exclusive &buf    const cell_Buffer *e = &buf;  qualifier lost
//     let exclusive e = &mut buf          cell_Buffer *e = &buf;        correct
//     let shared s = buf                  cell_Buffer s = buf;          COPY
//     let shared s = &buf                 const cell_Buffer *s = &buf;  correct
//     let copy snap = <a live borrow>     cell_Buffer *snap = e;        ALIAS
//
// The first cost a lost write, silently: `bump(exclusive e)` then `buf.len`
// printed 37 where the LLVM and MLIR backends printed 42, with the REFERENCE
// backend on the wrong side. The last aliased the lender, so a `copy`
// snapshot saw every later write where LLVM loads through. Neither produced
// any diagnostic from anything.
//
// WHY A HOST C FILE. A lost write is visible in the arithmetic, but a `shared`
// binding that is secretly a COPY is not: Cell's own rules forbid mutating a
// lender while it is shared-borrowed, so no Cell-side read can tell a copy
// from a reference. Only the callee knows which address it was handed. That is
// the same reasoning write_through_host.c records, and `distinct()` is the
// same instrument: it counts the distinct addresses handed over, so a spelling
// that copies raises it whether or not anything writes.
//
// WHY THE READING OF `buf.len` COMES BEFORE THE LAST BORROW, which is not
// cosmetic. A named loan ends early only when its holder is provably never
// reached again, and the liveness window whitelists exactly ONE position, a
// direct call argument (AGENTS.md, OWNERSHIP.md 0.3). `let copy snap = e6`
// mentions `e6` somewhere else, which makes that loan ineligible, so it lasts
// to the end of the block and every later read of `buf` is refused. Measured,
// not guessed: writing this file the other way round produced
// "cannot use 'buf.len' while it is exclusively borrowed". The snapshot shape
// therefore has to come last, which is the same order write_through_named.cell
// arrived at for the same reason.
//
// WHAT IT PRINTS, and how to read a wrong answer. One integer, `14242`, which
// decomposes as `distinct() | snap.len | len_before`:
//
//   len_before  42   37 plus one per `grow`, and five run before it is read.
//   snap.len    42   the value BEFORE the sixth grow, because `copy` is a
//                    snapshot. An aliasing `snap` reads 43.
//   distinct     1   every one of the nine calls handed over `&buf` itself.
//
//   A unique spelling whose write is LOST costs 1 from `len_before` AND adds 1
//   to `distinct`, because a copy is a different address: 24141 for one broken
//   spelling, 34040 for two. A SHARED spelling that copies moves `distinct`
//   alone, since `look` writes nothing: 24242. An aliasing `snap` moves only
//   the middle field: 14342. So the leading digit counts broken spellings of
//   either kind, and the other two fields say which kind.
//
// EXPECT-OUTPUT: 14242

pub struct Buffer {
    copy len: Int,
}

pub fn print_int(copy value: Int);

// Defined in let_binding_modes_host.c. `grow` records the address it was
// handed and then increments through it; `look` records the address and
// touches nothing, which is exactly what `shared` promises. `distinct` is how
// many different addresses the two of them have seen.
pub fn grow(exclusive b: Buffer);
pub fn look(shared b: Buffer);
pub fn distinct() -> Int;

pub fn main() {
    var owned buf = Buffer { len: 37 }

    // All five unique-borrow spellings that examples/borrows.cell declares
    // identical, each parked in a NAME before the call. `exclusive buf` is the
    // bare keyword form and reaches a backend as a bare reference, because
    // lowering consumes the written prefix; the four sigil forms arrive
    // wrapped in a unary borrow node. `exclusive &buf` mixes the two, and
    // borrows.cell states the rule for that: the KEYWORD WINS.
    //
    // Each loan ends at its `grow`, which is the one position the named-loan
    // liveness window whitelists (AGENTS.md, OWNERSHIP.md 0.3), so the next
    // binding may borrow `buf` again.
    let exclusive e1 = exclusive buf
    grow(exclusive e1)
    let exclusive e2 = &mut buf
    grow(exclusive e2)
    let exclusive e3 = &var buf
    grow(exclusive e3)
    let exclusive e4 = &exclusive buf
    grow(exclusive e4)
    let exclusive e5 = exclusive &buf
    grow(exclusive e5)

    // All three shared spellings, for the same reason. `look` writes nothing,
    // so these are measurable only through `distinct`.
    let shared s1 = buf
    look(shared s1)
    let shared s2 = shared buf
    look(shared s2)
    let shared s3 = &buf
    look(shared s3)

    // Read `buf` while it is still readable; see the header for why this
    // cannot come after the borrow below.
    let copy len_before = buf.len

    // And the other direction: a `copy` binding taken FROM a live borrow is a
    // snapshot of the object, never a second name for it. The grow after it
    // must not be visible in `snap`.
    let exclusive e6 = &mut buf
    let copy snap = e6
    grow(exclusive e6)

    print_int(len_before + 100 * snap.len + 10000 * distinct())
}
